1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,834
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,014 --> 00:00:04,144
Most money mistakes don't come from bad

3
00:00:04,144 --> 00:00:04,417
luck.

4
00:00:04,597 --> 00:00:06,257
They come from bad ideas.

5
00:00:06,437 --> 00:00:08,639
Things you heard from a parent,

6
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:10,201
a coworker, a TV show,

7
00:00:10,201 --> 00:00:11,976
or a video like this one,

8
00:00:11,976 --> 00:00:13,254
that sounded true,

9
00:00:13,254 --> 00:00:16,236
and quietly cost you thousands of dollars.

10
00:00:16,416 --> 00:00:18,745
Today, we're going to break twenty money

11
00:00:18,745 --> 00:00:20,375
myths that keep people poor.

12
00:00:20,725 --> 00:00:21,492
Twenty myths.

13
00:00:21,672 --> 00:00:22,965
Some are about math,

14
00:00:22,965 --> 00:00:24,452
some are about mindset,

15
00:00:24,452 --> 00:00:26,844
and some are about the everyday moves

16
00:00:26,844 --> 00:00:28,396
people make with credit,

17
00:00:28,396 --> 00:00:29,625
debt and investing.

18
00:00:29,974 --> 00:00:32,029
Here's why myths are so dangerous.

19
00:00:32,209 --> 00:00:34,160
A bad habit you know is bad.

20
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,280
You can work on it.

21
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:37,689
A myth feels like common sense.

22
00:00:37,869 --> 00:00:39,183
You don't question it,

23
00:00:39,183 --> 00:00:40,795
so you repeat it for years.

24
00:00:40,975 --> 00:00:42,769
And because money compounds,

25
00:00:42,769 --> 00:00:45,076
a small wrong belief held for twenty

26
00:00:45,076 --> 00:00:47,126
years can cost far more than one

27
00:00:47,126 --> 00:00:47,895
big mistake.

28
00:00:48,244 --> 00:00:50,532
We'll go through them from three angles.

29
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:52,066
Angle one, the numbers:

30
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:54,185
myths that fall apart the moment you

31
00:00:54,185 --> 00:00:54,891
do the math.

32
00:00:55,071 --> 00:00:56,812
Angle two, the psychology:

33
00:00:56,812 --> 00:00:59,088
myths about money and who you are.

34
00:00:59,268 --> 00:01:00,274
And angle three,

35
00:01:00,274 --> 00:01:02,347
the playbook: myths about credit,

36
00:01:02,347 --> 00:01:04,797
debt and investing that change what you

37
00:01:04,797 --> 00:01:05,677
do this month.

38
00:01:06,026 --> 00:01:07,768
Some of these will surprise you.

39
00:01:07,948 --> 00:01:11,102
Number five, number eight and number fifteen

40
00:01:11,102 --> 00:01:12,894
surprise almost everyone.

41
00:01:13,074 --> 00:01:14,641
Let's start with the numbers.

42
00:01:14,991 --> 00:01:16,974
Every number here comes from a named

43
00:01:16,974 --> 00:01:17,360
source.

44
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:20,199
Social Security figures come from the Social

45
00:01:20,199 --> 00:01:21,650
Security Administration.

46
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:24,291
Tax refund data comes from the IRS.

47
00:01:24,471 --> 00:01:27,133
Credit score guidance comes from the Consumer

48
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:28,790
Financial Protection Bureau.

49
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:31,333
Growth examples use stated math,

50
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:34,508
like a seven percent average yearly return.

51
00:01:34,688 --> 00:01:36,095
That's an illustration,

52
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:36,952
not a promise.

53
00:01:37,132 --> 00:01:38,482
This is education,

54
00:01:38,482 --> 00:01:40,058
not financial advice.

55
00:01:40,407 --> 00:01:41,139
Angle one.

56
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:43,502
Myths that fall apart with simple math.

57
00:01:43,851 --> 00:01:44,989
Myth number one.

58
00:01:45,169 --> 00:01:46,853
Small amounts don't matter.

59
00:01:47,202 --> 00:01:48,898
This myth sounds reasonable.

60
00:01:49,078 --> 00:01:50,227
What's fifty dollars?

61
00:01:50,407 --> 00:01:51,754
It won't change my life.

62
00:01:51,934 --> 00:01:52,950
And on its own,

63
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:55,254
one fifty dollar decision doesn't.

64
00:01:55,434 --> 00:01:58,093
But money decisions aren't one time events.

65
00:01:58,273 --> 00:01:59,132
They repeat.

66
00:01:59,312 --> 00:02:01,507
Fifty dollars a week is twenty six

67
00:02:01,507 --> 00:02:02,992
hundred dollars a year.

68
00:02:03,172 --> 00:02:05,615
And if that money is invested instead

69
00:02:05,615 --> 00:02:07,793
of spent, it doesn't just add up.

70
00:02:07,973 --> 00:02:09,076
It compounds.

71
00:02:09,425 --> 00:02:10,842
Picture two coworkers.

72
00:02:11,022 --> 00:02:13,202
One spends an extra fifty dollars a

73
00:02:13,202 --> 00:02:15,445
week on things she barely remembers.

74
00:02:15,625 --> 00:02:17,831
The other invests it automatically.

75
00:02:18,011 --> 00:02:20,556
Neither feels richer or poorer week to

76
00:02:20,556 --> 00:02:20,890
week.

77
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:23,100
But after twenty or thirty years,

78
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:25,068
the gap between them can be life

79
00:02:25,068 --> 00:02:25,622
changing.

80
00:02:25,802 --> 00:02:28,745
Try this: pick one small recurring expense

81
00:02:28,745 --> 00:02:31,337
and redirect it to savings for ninety

82
00:02:31,337 --> 00:02:31,688
days.

83
00:02:31,868 --> 00:02:33,052
Watch the balance.

84
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:34,946
Where does this myth come from?

85
00:02:35,126 --> 00:02:37,749
Mostly from how slow the start feels.

86
00:02:37,929 --> 00:02:38,915
In the first year,

87
00:02:38,915 --> 00:02:41,106
fifty dollars a week invested might earn

88
00:02:41,106 --> 00:02:42,257
only a little growth,

89
00:02:42,257 --> 00:02:43,900
so it really does look like it

90
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:44,721
doesn't matter.

91
00:02:44,901 --> 00:02:46,640
The payoff shows up years later,

92
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,651
when you've stopped paying attention.

93
00:02:48,831 --> 00:02:50,693
Our brains judge a habit by what

94
00:02:50,693 --> 00:02:51,799
it does this month,

95
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:53,719
not what it does in twenty years.

96
00:02:53,899 --> 00:02:56,689
That's exactly why small amounts get ignored,

97
00:02:56,689 --> 00:02:58,921
and exactly why the people who don't

98
00:02:58,921 --> 00:03:00,470
ignore them end up ahead.

99
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:01,714
Here's the math.

100
00:03:01,894 --> 00:03:04,067
About two hundred dollars a month,

101
00:03:04,067 --> 00:03:06,752
invested at a seven percent average yearly

102
00:03:06,752 --> 00:03:08,350
return, for thirty years.

103
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:10,782
You'd put in seventy two thousand dollars.

104
00:03:10,962 --> 00:03:12,111
Look where it could end up.

105
00:03:12,461 --> 00:03:13,669
Myth number two.

106
00:03:13,849 --> 00:03:15,666
Cash is the safest place for your

107
00:03:15,666 --> 00:03:15,997
money.

108
00:03:16,346 --> 00:03:17,735
In the short term,

109
00:03:17,735 --> 00:03:18,738
cash is safe.

110
00:03:18,918 --> 00:03:20,657
It won't drop twenty percent in a

111
00:03:20,657 --> 00:03:20,973
month.

112
00:03:21,153 --> 00:03:23,409
That's exactly why it's perfect for an

113
00:03:23,409 --> 00:03:24,299
emergency fund.

114
00:03:24,479 --> 00:03:25,764
But over decades,

115
00:03:25,764 --> 00:03:27,578
cash has a hidden enemy:

116
00:03:27,578 --> 00:03:28,334
inflation.

117
00:03:28,514 --> 00:03:30,162
Prices rise over time,

118
00:03:30,162 --> 00:03:32,334
so the same dollar buys less.

119
00:03:32,514 --> 00:03:34,296
At three percent inflation,

120
00:03:34,296 --> 00:03:36,079
by the rule of seventy two,

121
00:03:36,079 --> 00:03:38,851
prices roughly double in about twenty four

122
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:39,247
years.

123
00:03:39,598 --> 00:03:42,222
That means one hundred thousand dollars sitting

124
00:03:42,222 --> 00:03:44,288
in a zero interest account for twenty

125
00:03:44,288 --> 00:03:46,299
four years could buy only about half

126
00:03:46,299 --> 00:03:47,527
of what it buys today.

127
00:03:47,707 --> 00:03:49,170
The balance looks the same.

128
00:03:49,350 --> 00:03:50,488
The value isn't.

129
00:03:50,668 --> 00:03:52,657
Cash is safe from crashes,

130
00:03:52,657 --> 00:03:54,035
but not from time.

131
00:03:54,215 --> 00:03:56,762
Try this: keep your emergency fund in

132
00:03:56,762 --> 00:03:58,897
cash, ideally earning interest,

133
00:03:58,897 --> 00:04:01,169
and let your long term money grow

134
00:04:01,169 --> 00:04:04,887
in investments that have historically beaten inflation

135
00:04:04,887 --> 00:04:06,126
over long periods.

136
00:04:06,477 --> 00:04:08,566
This myth comes from real pain.

137
00:04:08,746 --> 00:04:11,703
Many people watched their parents lose money

138
00:04:11,703 --> 00:04:12,443
in a crash,

139
00:04:12,443 --> 00:04:14,123
or lost money themselves,

140
00:04:14,123 --> 00:04:15,736
and decided never again.

141
00:04:15,916 --> 00:04:17,599
That fear is understandable.

142
00:04:17,779 --> 00:04:19,639
But it swaps a visible risk,

143
00:04:19,639 --> 00:04:21,631
a crash, for an invisible one,

144
00:04:21,631 --> 00:04:22,295
inflation.

145
00:04:22,475 --> 00:04:25,219
The answer isn't all cash or all

146
00:04:25,219 --> 00:04:25,819
stocks.

147
00:04:25,999 --> 00:04:28,298
It's matching each dollar to its job.

148
00:04:28,478 --> 00:04:31,219
Money for emergencies and near term goals

149
00:04:31,219 --> 00:04:32,088
stays stable.

150
00:04:32,268 --> 00:04:34,496
Money for twenty years from now can

151
00:04:34,496 --> 00:04:35,450
take more risk,

152
00:04:35,450 --> 00:04:37,423
because it has time to recover.

153
00:04:37,774 --> 00:04:39,155
Myth number three.

154
00:04:39,335 --> 00:04:41,344
Your house is your best investment.

155
00:04:41,694 --> 00:04:43,491
A home can be a great thing

156
00:04:43,491 --> 00:04:43,957
to own.

157
00:04:44,137 --> 00:04:45,535
It gives you stability,

158
00:04:45,535 --> 00:04:48,027
and paying down a mortgage builds equity.

159
00:04:48,207 --> 00:04:51,004
But calling it your best investment ignores

160
00:04:51,004 --> 00:04:51,655
the costs.

161
00:04:51,835 --> 00:04:53,872
Property taxes, insurance,

162
00:04:53,872 --> 00:04:57,632
maintenance, repairs, closing costs when you buy

163
00:04:57,632 --> 00:04:58,337
and sell.

164
00:04:58,517 --> 00:05:00,640
Many planners use a rule of thumb

165
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,828
of setting aside around one to two

166
00:05:02,828 --> 00:05:05,080
percent of a home's value each year

167
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:06,109
just for upkeep.

168
00:05:06,459 --> 00:05:09,311
And historically, home prices have grown more

169
00:05:09,311 --> 00:05:11,783
slowly than many people assume once you

170
00:05:11,783 --> 00:05:14,444
account for inflation and all those costs,

171
00:05:14,444 --> 00:05:16,662
though it varies hugely by city and

172
00:05:16,662 --> 00:05:17,106
decade.

173
00:05:17,286 --> 00:05:18,925
The best way to see a home

174
00:05:18,925 --> 00:05:20,565
is as a place to live that

175
00:05:20,565 --> 00:05:21,952
may also build wealth,

176
00:05:21,952 --> 00:05:23,718
not as your retirement plan.

177
00:05:23,898 --> 00:05:25,734
Try this: add up what your home

178
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:27,334
really costs you each year,

179
00:05:27,334 --> 00:05:28,519
beyond the mortgage.

180
00:05:28,699 --> 00:05:31,125
Taxes, insurance, repairs.

181
00:05:31,305 --> 00:05:33,035
Most people are surprised.

182
00:05:33,384 --> 00:05:35,242
Why do so many people believe it?

183
00:05:35,422 --> 00:05:37,032
Because for many families,

184
00:05:37,032 --> 00:05:39,323
the house really was the biggest pile

185
00:05:39,323 --> 00:05:40,809
of wealth they ever had.

186
00:05:40,989 --> 00:05:43,672
But often that's because the mortgage forced

187
00:05:43,672 --> 00:05:45,197
them to save every month,

188
00:05:45,197 --> 00:05:47,575
not because the house was a spectacular

189
00:05:47,575 --> 00:05:48,245
investment.

190
00:05:48,425 --> 00:05:50,928
The mortgage payment was automatic and non

191
00:05:50,928 --> 00:05:51,583
negotiable.

192
00:05:51,763 --> 00:05:53,110
That's the real lesson.

193
00:05:53,290 --> 00:05:55,624
Forced, automatic saving works.

194
00:05:55,804 --> 00:05:57,624
You can get the same effect with

195
00:05:57,624 --> 00:06:00,809
automatic investing, without putting everything into one

196
00:06:00,809 --> 00:06:02,003
property in one city.

197
00:06:02,353 --> 00:06:03,654
Myth number four.

198
00:06:03,834 --> 00:06:05,633
Renting is throwing money away.

199
00:06:05,983 --> 00:06:07,783
Rent pays for a place to live.

200
00:06:07,963 --> 00:06:09,646
That's not throwing money away.

201
00:06:09,826 --> 00:06:11,104
It's buying shelter.

202
00:06:11,284 --> 00:06:13,853
Owners also pay for things they never

203
00:06:13,853 --> 00:06:15,797
get back: mortgage interest,

204
00:06:15,797 --> 00:06:18,714
property taxes, insurance and maintenance.

205
00:06:18,894 --> 00:06:21,231
The real question is not rent versus

206
00:06:21,231 --> 00:06:21,750
nothing.

207
00:06:21,930 --> 00:06:24,696
It's rent versus the unrecoverable costs of

208
00:06:24,696 --> 00:06:25,146
owning.

209
00:06:25,496 --> 00:06:27,221
In some cities and some years,

210
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:29,808
renting and investing the difference can come

211
00:06:29,808 --> 00:06:30,383
out ahead.

212
00:06:30,563 --> 00:06:32,203
In others, buying wins,

213
00:06:32,203 --> 00:06:34,627
especially if you stay many years.

214
00:06:34,807 --> 00:06:36,026
It depends on prices,

215
00:06:36,026 --> 00:06:38,174
interest rates, how long you'll stay,

216
00:06:38,174 --> 00:06:40,902
and whether you actually invest the difference.

217
00:06:41,082 --> 00:06:42,545
Try this: before buying,

218
00:06:42,545 --> 00:06:44,922
compare your rent with the full monthly

219
00:06:44,922 --> 00:06:45,836
cost of owning,

220
00:06:45,836 --> 00:06:47,177
not just the mortgage.

221
00:06:47,357 --> 00:06:49,137
Then decide with numbers,

222
00:06:49,137 --> 00:06:50,062
not a slogan.

223
00:06:50,413 --> 00:06:52,710
This myth is often repeated by people

224
00:06:52,710 --> 00:06:54,449
selling homes and mortgages,

225
00:06:54,449 --> 00:06:56,747
and by parents who bought decades ago

226
00:06:56,747 --> 00:06:59,293
when prices relative to income were often

227
00:06:59,293 --> 00:06:59,666
lower.

228
00:06:59,846 --> 00:07:02,027
Renting has real advantages:

229
00:07:02,027 --> 00:07:04,909
flexibility to move for a better job,

230
00:07:04,909 --> 00:07:06,856
no surprise repair bills,

231
00:07:06,856 --> 00:07:08,960
and a smaller upfront cost.

232
00:07:09,140 --> 00:07:10,374
Owning has its own:

233
00:07:10,374 --> 00:07:13,364
stability, control, and a payment that doesn't

234
00:07:13,364 --> 00:07:15,509
rise with the market if your rate

235
00:07:15,509 --> 00:07:16,094
is fixed.

236
00:07:16,274 --> 00:07:18,039
Neither is automatically smarter.

237
00:07:18,219 --> 00:07:20,020
The smartest choice is the one that

238
00:07:20,020 --> 00:07:21,667
fits your life and your numbers.

239
00:07:22,017 --> 00:07:23,421
Myth number five.

240
00:07:23,601 --> 00:07:26,364
Social Security will cover your retirement.

241
00:07:26,715 --> 00:07:28,653
This is one of the most expensive

242
00:07:28,653 --> 00:07:29,652
myths in America.

243
00:07:29,832 --> 00:07:33,161
According to the Social Security Administration,

244
00:07:33,161 --> 00:07:34,757
for the average worker,

245
00:07:34,757 --> 00:07:38,364
retirement benefits replace only about forty percent

246
00:07:38,364 --> 00:07:40,861
of their earnings before retirement.

247
00:07:41,041 --> 00:07:44,310
Most financial planners say people need considerably

248
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:46,322
more than that to keep a similar

249
00:07:46,322 --> 00:07:46,951
lifestyle.

250
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:49,681
Social Security is valuable.

251
00:07:49,861 --> 00:07:51,957
It's protected against inflation,

252
00:07:51,957 --> 00:07:53,355
and it lasts for life.

253
00:07:53,535 --> 00:07:55,803
But it was designed as a foundation,

254
00:07:55,803 --> 00:07:56,937
not a full income.

255
00:07:57,117 --> 00:07:59,659
The gap has to come from somewhere:

256
00:07:59,659 --> 00:08:01,257
workplace plans, IRAs,

257
00:08:01,257 --> 00:08:03,653
other savings, or working longer.

258
00:08:03,833 --> 00:08:05,885
Try this: create an account on the

259
00:08:05,885 --> 00:08:08,541
official Social Security website and look at

260
00:08:08,541 --> 00:08:09,929
your estimated benefit.

261
00:08:10,109 --> 00:08:11,724
Then compare it with what you spend

262
00:08:11,724 --> 00:08:12,001
today.

263
00:08:12,351 --> 00:08:15,549
The myth survives partly because older generations

264
00:08:15,549 --> 00:08:17,788
often had pensions on top of Social

265
00:08:17,788 --> 00:08:21,307
Security, guaranteed monthly checks from their employer

266
00:08:21,307 --> 00:08:21,882
for life.

267
00:08:22,062 --> 00:08:25,011
Today, far fewer private sector workers have

268
00:08:25,011 --> 00:08:26,486
a traditional pension.

269
00:08:26,666 --> 00:08:28,250
Most have a four oh one k

270
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:29,390
or nothing at all,

271
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:32,051
which means the responsibility for the gap

272
00:08:32,051 --> 00:08:34,015
moved from the employer to you.

273
00:08:34,195 --> 00:08:36,942
If your parents retired comfortably on Social

274
00:08:36,942 --> 00:08:38,408
Security plus a pension,

275
00:08:38,408 --> 00:08:41,033
your plan probably needs to look different.

276
00:08:41,384 --> 00:08:44,981
The Social Security Administration says benefits replace

277
00:08:44,981 --> 00:08:47,679
about forty percent of an average worker's

278
00:08:47,679 --> 00:08:49,220
pre retirement earnings.

279
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:51,100
The rest has to come from your

280
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:51,780
own savings.

281
00:08:52,131 --> 00:08:53,663
Myth number six.

282
00:08:53,843 --> 00:08:55,886
The tax refund is free money.

283
00:08:56,237 --> 00:08:58,837
A tax refund feels like a bonus.

284
00:08:59,017 --> 00:09:00,367
But it's not a gift from the

285
00:09:00,367 --> 00:09:00,898
government.

286
00:09:01,078 --> 00:09:02,252
It's your own money,

287
00:09:02,252 --> 00:09:04,306
which you overpaid during the year,

288
00:09:04,306 --> 00:09:06,419
coming back to you without interest.

289
00:09:06,599 --> 00:09:08,854
IRS data shows the average refund in

290
00:09:08,854 --> 00:09:11,548
recent filing seasons has been around three

291
00:09:11,548 --> 00:09:12,613
thousand dollars.

292
00:09:12,793 --> 00:09:14,995
That's roughly two hundred fifty dollars a

293
00:09:14,995 --> 00:09:17,092
month that people lent to the government

294
00:09:17,092 --> 00:09:17,564
for free.

295
00:09:17,915 --> 00:09:19,010
For some people,

296
00:09:19,010 --> 00:09:21,544
a big refund works as forced savings,

297
00:09:21,544 --> 00:09:22,640
and that's okay.

298
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:24,960
But if you're carrying credit card debt

299
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:26,991
at twenty percent while waiting for a

300
00:09:26,991 --> 00:09:28,857
refund, that's an expensive trade.

301
00:09:29,037 --> 00:09:30,730
Try this: if you get a large

302
00:09:30,730 --> 00:09:31,818
refund every year,

303
00:09:31,818 --> 00:09:35,144
consider adjusting your withholding with your employer.

304
00:09:35,324 --> 00:09:38,094
Then send that extra monthly money straight

305
00:09:38,094 --> 00:09:39,318
to debt or savings.

306
00:09:39,668 --> 00:09:42,044
Refund season is also a big season

307
00:09:42,044 --> 00:09:45,399
for retailers, car dealers and travel companies,

308
00:09:45,399 --> 00:09:46,587
and they know it.

309
00:09:46,767 --> 00:09:48,845
Ads land right when the money does.

310
00:09:49,025 --> 00:09:51,858
That's one reason refunds disappear so fast.

311
00:09:52,038 --> 00:09:54,106
A simple defense is to decide the

312
00:09:54,106 --> 00:09:56,049
refund's job before it arrives,

313
00:09:56,049 --> 00:09:57,929
and move it there the same day

314
00:09:57,929 --> 00:09:58,493
it lands.

315
00:09:58,673 --> 00:10:00,618
The less time it sits in checking,

316
00:10:00,618 --> 00:10:02,162
the less likely it is to be

317
00:10:02,162 --> 00:10:04,449
spent on something you won't remember by

318
00:10:04,449 --> 00:10:04,850
summer.

319
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:06,720
Myth number seven.

320
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:08,752
You can wait for the perfect moment

321
00:10:08,752 --> 00:10:09,280
to invest.

322
00:10:09,631 --> 00:10:11,488
Waiting for the dip sounds smart.

323
00:10:11,668 --> 00:10:13,340
Buy low, sell high.

324
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:15,979
The problem is that nobody knows when

325
00:10:15,979 --> 00:10:17,176
the dip will come,

326
00:10:17,176 --> 00:10:18,505
how deep it will be,

327
00:10:18,505 --> 00:10:19,569
or when it ends.

328
00:10:19,749 --> 00:10:22,253
Many of the market's best days have

329
00:10:22,253 --> 00:10:25,187
historically come very close to its worst

330
00:10:25,187 --> 00:10:27,620
days, during the scariest periods.

331
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:29,017
If you're out waiting,

332
00:10:29,017 --> 00:10:30,122
you often miss both.

333
00:10:30,473 --> 00:10:33,259
Studies of long term returns have repeatedly

334
00:10:33,259 --> 00:10:35,539
found that missing even a handful of

335
00:10:35,539 --> 00:10:37,945
the best days over decades can sharply

336
00:10:37,945 --> 00:10:39,528
reduce your total return.

337
00:10:39,708 --> 00:10:42,269
Time in the market has generally beaten

338
00:10:42,269 --> 00:10:43,451
timing the market,

339
00:10:43,451 --> 00:10:45,618
though no strategy is guaranteed.

340
00:10:45,798 --> 00:10:47,765
Try this: instead of waiting,

341
00:10:47,765 --> 00:10:49,937
invest a fixed amount on a fixed

342
00:10:49,937 --> 00:10:51,022
day every month.

343
00:10:51,202 --> 00:10:53,246
It's called dollar cost averaging,

344
00:10:53,246 --> 00:10:54,929
and it removes the guessing.

345
00:10:55,279 --> 00:10:57,264
This myth is fed by stories.

346
00:10:57,444 --> 00:10:59,866
Everybody has heard about someone who sold

347
00:10:59,866 --> 00:11:01,078
right before a crash.

348
00:11:01,258 --> 00:11:03,559
Fewer people hear about the many who

349
00:11:03,559 --> 00:11:04,517
sold too early,

350
00:11:04,517 --> 00:11:06,499
or never got back in and missed

351
00:11:06,499 --> 00:11:07,330
the recovery.

352
00:11:07,510 --> 00:11:09,797
Being right once makes a great story.

353
00:11:09,977 --> 00:11:11,562
Being right over and over,

354
00:11:11,562 --> 00:11:13,207
on both the exit and the re

355
00:11:13,207 --> 00:11:16,377
entry, is something even many professionals struggle

356
00:11:16,377 --> 00:11:17,535
to do consistently.

357
00:11:17,715 --> 00:11:20,300
A boring schedule beats a brilliant guess

358
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:21,372
most of the time.

359
00:11:21,723 --> 00:11:23,127
That's the math myths.

360
00:11:23,307 --> 00:11:24,642
Now the harder ones.

361
00:11:24,822 --> 00:11:26,285
The myths that live in your head.

362
00:11:26,636 --> 00:11:27,622
Angle two.

363
00:11:27,802 --> 00:11:29,881
Myths about money and mindset.

364
00:11:30,061 --> 00:11:31,402
These are harder to see,

365
00:11:31,402 --> 00:11:33,358
because they feel like who you are.

366
00:11:33,708 --> 00:11:34,938
Myth number eight.

367
00:11:35,118 --> 00:11:37,649
More income will fix my money problems.

368
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,520
Higher income helps.

369
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:41,255
But it doesn't fix habits,

370
00:11:41,255 --> 00:11:42,510
and history shows it.

371
00:11:42,690 --> 00:11:45,249
A well known study by economists looking

372
00:11:45,249 --> 00:11:48,511
at professional football players found that roughly

373
00:11:48,511 --> 00:11:50,942
one in six filed for bankruptcy within

374
00:11:50,942 --> 00:11:52,925
about twelve years of retiring,

375
00:11:52,925 --> 00:11:55,867
despite earning millions during their careers.

376
00:11:56,047 --> 00:11:58,207
Big income, no system.

377
00:11:58,557 --> 00:12:00,128
You don't have to be a pro

378
00:12:00,128 --> 00:12:01,215
athlete to see it.

379
00:12:01,395 --> 00:12:02,968
Many people get a raise,

380
00:12:02,968 --> 00:12:04,147
and within a year,

381
00:12:04,147 --> 00:12:06,505
the extra money has been absorbed by

382
00:12:06,505 --> 00:12:07,291
a nicer car,

383
00:12:07,291 --> 00:12:09,650
a bigger place and more dinners out.

384
00:12:09,830 --> 00:12:11,618
Their stress stays the same.

385
00:12:11,798 --> 00:12:13,433
The fix is to build habits at

386
00:12:13,433 --> 00:12:14,560
your current income,

387
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:16,364
so that when more money arrives,

388
00:12:16,364 --> 00:12:17,661
it has somewhere to go.

389
00:12:17,841 --> 00:12:20,244
Try this: decide now what percent of

390
00:12:20,244 --> 00:12:22,380
your next raise goes to savings.

391
00:12:22,731 --> 00:12:23,950
Why do we believe it?

392
00:12:24,130 --> 00:12:26,072
Because when money is tight,

393
00:12:26,072 --> 00:12:28,084
more income really does help,

394
00:12:28,084 --> 00:12:29,749
and that feeling sticks.

395
00:12:29,929 --> 00:12:32,471
We assume the relief scales forever.

396
00:12:32,651 --> 00:12:34,628
But spending has a way of rising

397
00:12:34,628 --> 00:12:35,554
to meet income.

398
00:12:35,734 --> 00:12:38,544
Researchers call it lifestyle inflation.

399
00:12:38,724 --> 00:12:40,828
The people who escape it usually do

400
00:12:40,828 --> 00:12:43,173
one thing: they decide in advance where

401
00:12:43,173 --> 00:12:44,075
new money goes,

402
00:12:44,075 --> 00:12:46,119
before they get used to having it.

403
00:12:46,299 --> 00:12:47,518
Income is fuel.

404
00:12:47,698 --> 00:12:48,871
Habits are the engine.

405
00:12:49,221 --> 00:12:50,567
Myth number nine.

406
00:12:50,747 --> 00:12:53,259
Rich people just got lucky or inherited

407
00:12:53,259 --> 00:12:53,453
it.

408
00:12:53,803 --> 00:12:54,406
Some did.

409
00:12:54,586 --> 00:12:56,444
Luck and family money are real.

410
00:12:56,624 --> 00:12:59,426
But researchers Thomas Stanley and William Danko,

411
00:12:59,426 --> 00:13:01,714
in their book The Millionaire Next Door,

412
00:13:01,714 --> 00:13:04,231
found that most of the American millionaires

413
00:13:04,231 --> 00:13:06,690
they studied were first generation wealthy.

414
00:13:06,870 --> 00:13:09,940
Many were business owners or professionals who

415
00:13:09,940 --> 00:13:11,541
lived below their means,

416
00:13:11,541 --> 00:13:14,544
drove ordinary cars and invested steadily for

417
00:13:14,544 --> 00:13:15,078
decades.

418
00:13:15,428 --> 00:13:17,877
This myth is dangerous because it makes

419
00:13:17,877 --> 00:13:19,259
effort feel pointless.

420
00:13:19,439 --> 00:13:20,820
If it's all luck,

421
00:13:20,820 --> 00:13:21,471
why try?

422
00:13:21,651 --> 00:13:23,980
But the habits Stanley described are boring

423
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:25,552
and available to most people.

424
00:13:25,732 --> 00:13:27,067
Spend less than you earn.

425
00:13:27,247 --> 00:13:28,489
Avoid showing off.

426
00:13:28,669 --> 00:13:30,132
Invest consistently.

427
00:13:30,312 --> 00:13:32,511
Try this: next time you see someone

428
00:13:32,511 --> 00:13:34,836
with wealth, ask what habits might be

429
00:13:34,836 --> 00:13:36,721
behind it, not just what luck.

430
00:13:37,071 --> 00:13:38,995
Part of this myth comes from what

431
00:13:38,995 --> 00:13:39,636
we can see.

432
00:13:39,816 --> 00:13:42,511
Visible wealth is often the flashy kind,

433
00:13:42,511 --> 00:13:44,263
new money spending loudly.

434
00:13:44,443 --> 00:13:47,009
Quiet wealth is invisible by design.

435
00:13:47,189 --> 00:13:49,059
The teacher with a paid off house

436
00:13:49,059 --> 00:13:51,497
and a large retirement account doesn't post

437
00:13:51,497 --> 00:13:52,007
about it.

438
00:13:52,187 --> 00:13:53,974
So our picture of rich people is

439
00:13:53,974 --> 00:13:55,929
skewed toward the ones who show it,

440
00:13:55,929 --> 00:13:57,716
and those are often not the ones

441
00:13:57,716 --> 00:13:58,387
who keep it.

442
00:13:58,737 --> 00:14:00,083
Myth number ten.

443
00:14:00,263 --> 00:14:01,877
Budgets are for broke people.

444
00:14:02,227 --> 00:14:04,423
Lots of people think a budget means

445
00:14:04,423 --> 00:14:05,176
deprivation.

446
00:14:05,356 --> 00:14:06,863
Counting every penny,

447
00:14:06,863 --> 00:14:08,084
never having fun.

448
00:14:08,264 --> 00:14:09,758
But a budget is just a plan

449
00:14:09,758 --> 00:14:11,584
for your money before it arrives.

450
00:14:11,764 --> 00:14:15,255
Wealthy households and businesses budget all the

451
00:14:15,255 --> 00:14:15,619
time.

452
00:14:15,799 --> 00:14:16,708
Without a plan,

453
00:14:16,708 --> 00:14:19,073
money tends to drift toward whatever is

454
00:14:19,073 --> 00:14:20,164
loudest this week.

455
00:14:20,515 --> 00:14:22,639
A good budget can be very simple.

456
00:14:22,819 --> 00:14:26,361
One popular approach splits after tax income

457
00:14:26,361 --> 00:14:28,775
into needs, wants and savings.

458
00:14:28,955 --> 00:14:31,420
Another just automates savings first and lets

459
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:32,844
you spend the rest freely.

460
00:14:33,024 --> 00:14:34,244
The point isn't the method.

461
00:14:34,424 --> 00:14:35,434
It's the decision.

462
00:14:35,614 --> 00:14:37,878
Try this: write three numbers.

463
00:14:38,058 --> 00:14:39,611
What you need each month,

464
00:14:39,611 --> 00:14:40,978
what you want to save,

465
00:14:40,978 --> 00:14:42,469
and what's left for fun.

466
00:14:42,649 --> 00:14:43,392
That's a budget.

467
00:14:43,743 --> 00:14:46,172
The myth often comes from bad budget

468
00:14:46,172 --> 00:14:46,982
experiences.

469
00:14:47,162 --> 00:14:49,786
Someone tried tracking every coffee in a

470
00:14:49,786 --> 00:14:52,214
spreadsheet, felt guilty for a month,

471
00:14:52,214 --> 00:14:52,804
and quit.

472
00:14:52,984 --> 00:14:54,354
That's not a budget problem.

473
00:14:54,534 --> 00:14:55,788
That's a design problem.

474
00:14:55,968 --> 00:14:57,884
The best budget is the one you

475
00:14:57,884 --> 00:14:59,991
barely notice: automatic savings,

476
00:14:59,991 --> 00:15:01,523
automatic bill payments,

477
00:15:01,523 --> 00:15:03,631
and a clear amount you're free to

478
00:15:03,631 --> 00:15:05,929
spend without tracking every dollar.

479
00:15:06,109 --> 00:15:08,257
Less effort, more results.

480
00:15:08,608 --> 00:15:10,082
Myth number eleven.

481
00:15:10,262 --> 00:15:11,969
Talking about money is rude.

482
00:15:12,319 --> 00:15:14,466
Many families never talk about money.

483
00:15:14,646 --> 00:15:16,940
Kids grow up not knowing what things

484
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:18,278
cost, how debt works,

485
00:15:18,278 --> 00:15:19,999
or how their parents saved.

486
00:15:20,179 --> 00:15:21,927
Coworkers don't share salaries,

487
00:15:21,927 --> 00:15:24,184
so they don't know if they're underpaid.

488
00:15:24,364 --> 00:15:27,005
Couples avoid the topic until it becomes

489
00:15:27,005 --> 00:15:27,534
a fight.

490
00:15:27,714 --> 00:15:29,253
Silence feels polite,

491
00:15:29,253 --> 00:15:31,673
but it mostly protects bad deals.

492
00:15:32,023 --> 00:15:33,775
You don't need to announce your net

493
00:15:33,775 --> 00:15:34,577
worth at dinner.

494
00:15:34,757 --> 00:15:36,920
But talking openly with a partner,

495
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:39,784
sharing salary ranges with trusted coworkers,

496
00:15:39,784 --> 00:15:42,138
and teaching kids how money works can

497
00:15:42,138 --> 00:15:43,093
be worth a lot.

498
00:15:43,273 --> 00:15:46,001
Try this: have one money conversation this

499
00:15:46,001 --> 00:15:46,326
week.

500
00:15:46,506 --> 00:15:47,518
With a partner,

501
00:15:47,518 --> 00:15:49,002
a friend or your kids.

502
00:15:49,182 --> 00:15:50,169
Keep it simple.

503
00:15:50,349 --> 00:15:52,100
What's one money thing you wish you'd

504
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:52,857
learned earlier?

505
00:15:53,207 --> 00:15:56,051
This myth has real costs at work.

506
00:15:56,231 --> 00:15:58,833
When nobody knows what anyone else earns,

507
00:15:58,833 --> 00:16:01,244
it's hard to tell if you're underpaid,

508
00:16:01,244 --> 00:16:02,767
and harder to negotiate.

509
00:16:02,947 --> 00:16:04,110
In recent years,

510
00:16:04,110 --> 00:16:06,581
a number of states have passed pay

511
00:16:06,581 --> 00:16:10,287
transparency laws requiring many employers to share

512
00:16:10,287 --> 00:16:12,468
salary ranges in job postings.

513
00:16:12,648 --> 00:16:14,795
That's a sign the silence is breaking.

514
00:16:14,975 --> 00:16:15,533
Use it.

515
00:16:15,713 --> 00:16:17,561
Look up the posted ranges for your

516
00:16:17,561 --> 00:16:19,138
role before your next review.

517
00:16:19,488 --> 00:16:20,811
Myth number twelve.

518
00:16:20,991 --> 00:16:22,856
I'm too young to worry about it,

519
00:16:22,856 --> 00:16:24,021
or too old to start.

520
00:16:24,372 --> 00:16:25,373
In your twenties,

521
00:16:25,373 --> 00:16:27,611
retirement feels like science fiction.

522
00:16:27,791 --> 00:16:30,029
But that's exactly when time is most

523
00:16:30,029 --> 00:16:30,589
powerful.

524
00:16:30,769 --> 00:16:33,630
Money invested at twenty five has forty

525
00:16:33,630 --> 00:16:36,272
years to compound before sixty five.

526
00:16:36,452 --> 00:16:37,541
In your fifties,

527
00:16:37,541 --> 00:16:38,971
it can feel too late.

528
00:16:39,151 --> 00:16:41,209
But you may have fifteen or more

529
00:16:41,209 --> 00:16:42,430
years of work left,

530
00:16:42,430 --> 00:16:43,716
your highest income,

531
00:16:43,716 --> 00:16:46,352
and extra catch up contribution limits in

532
00:16:46,352 --> 00:16:47,638
retirement accounts.

533
00:16:47,988 --> 00:16:49,961
Both ages have a superpower.

534
00:16:50,141 --> 00:16:51,511
The young have time.

535
00:16:51,691 --> 00:16:52,910
The older have income.

536
00:16:53,090 --> 00:16:55,136
The only real mistake is waiting for

537
00:16:55,136 --> 00:16:56,840
a better age that never comes.

538
00:16:57,020 --> 00:16:58,671
Try this: if you're young,

539
00:16:58,671 --> 00:17:01,084
start with any amount and automate it.

540
00:17:01,264 --> 00:17:02,231
If you're older,

541
00:17:02,231 --> 00:17:04,831
find out your current catch up contribution

542
00:17:04,831 --> 00:17:06,765
limits, and aim to save a bigger

543
00:17:06,765 --> 00:17:07,974
share of each raise.

544
00:17:08,325 --> 00:17:10,659
Both versions of this myth are really

545
00:17:10,659 --> 00:17:11,606
the same thing:

546
00:17:11,606 --> 00:17:12,678
a reason to wait.

547
00:17:12,858 --> 00:17:14,240
Too young means not yet.

548
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:16,254
Too old means not anymore.

549
00:17:16,434 --> 00:17:19,134
Either way, the result is the same,

550
00:17:19,134 --> 00:17:19,906
no action.

551
00:17:20,086 --> 00:17:21,887
The fix is to make the first

552
00:17:21,887 --> 00:17:23,817
step so small it's hard to say

553
00:17:23,817 --> 00:17:24,010
no.

554
00:17:24,190 --> 00:17:25,850
One percent of your paycheck.

555
00:17:26,030 --> 00:17:27,377
Twenty dollars a week.

556
00:17:27,557 --> 00:17:29,792
A single phone call to your benefits

557
00:17:29,792 --> 00:17:30,227
office.

558
00:17:30,407 --> 00:17:32,904
Momentum matters more than size at the

559
00:17:32,904 --> 00:17:33,298
start.

560
00:17:33,648 --> 00:17:35,447
Myth number thirteen.

561
00:17:35,627 --> 00:17:36,974
Being frugal means being cheap.

562
00:17:37,324 --> 00:17:39,708
Cheap is buying the lowest price every

563
00:17:39,708 --> 00:17:41,966
time, even when it costs more later,

564
00:17:41,966 --> 00:17:44,162
or making other people pay for you.

565
00:17:44,342 --> 00:17:45,747
Frugal is different.

566
00:17:45,927 --> 00:17:48,202
Frugal means spending deliberately.

567
00:17:48,382 --> 00:17:50,348
Cutting hard on things you don't care

568
00:17:50,348 --> 00:17:52,314
about, so you can spend generously on

569
00:17:52,314 --> 00:17:53,270
the things you do.

570
00:17:53,620 --> 00:17:55,908
A frugal person might drive a ten

571
00:17:55,908 --> 00:17:58,127
year old car and skip the latest

572
00:17:58,127 --> 00:18:00,901
phone, then spend happily on travel with

573
00:18:00,901 --> 00:18:01,387
family.

574
00:18:01,567 --> 00:18:04,144
A cheap person buys shoes that fall

575
00:18:04,144 --> 00:18:05,616
apart in six months,

576
00:18:05,616 --> 00:18:07,163
then buys them again.

577
00:18:07,343 --> 00:18:09,308
Frugality is about value,

578
00:18:09,308 --> 00:18:10,094
not price.

579
00:18:10,274 --> 00:18:13,196
Try this: list your top three priorities

580
00:18:13,196 --> 00:18:13,780
in life.

581
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:15,052
Then look at your spending.

582
00:18:15,232 --> 00:18:16,788
Does your money match the list?

583
00:18:17,138 --> 00:18:20,380
This myth survives because people confuse frugality

584
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:21,143
with misery.

585
00:18:21,323 --> 00:18:23,191
Nobody wants to be the friend who

586
00:18:23,191 --> 00:18:24,493
never pays their share.

587
00:18:24,673 --> 00:18:27,304
But real frugality looks generous on the

588
00:18:27,304 --> 00:18:27,830
outside.

589
00:18:28,010 --> 00:18:30,210
The frugal person can host dinner at

590
00:18:30,210 --> 00:18:32,286
home, help a friend in trouble and

591
00:18:32,286 --> 00:18:34,180
give to causes they care about,

592
00:18:34,180 --> 00:18:37,173
because they're not spending everything on things

593
00:18:37,173 --> 00:18:38,761
that don't matter to them.

594
00:18:39,112 --> 00:18:40,795
Myth number fourteen.

595
00:18:40,975 --> 00:18:42,926
Investing is just gambling.

596
00:18:43,276 --> 00:18:45,529
Gambling has a built in negative expected

597
00:18:45,529 --> 00:18:46,793
outcome for the player.

598
00:18:46,973 --> 00:18:48,471
The house wins over time.

599
00:18:48,651 --> 00:18:49,769
Owning a broad,

600
00:18:49,769 --> 00:18:53,348
diversified slice of many businesses for decades

601
00:18:53,348 --> 00:18:54,317
is different.

602
00:18:54,497 --> 00:18:56,027
Companies produce goods,

603
00:18:56,027 --> 00:18:57,367
services and profits,

604
00:18:57,367 --> 00:18:59,918
and over long periods broad markets have

605
00:18:59,918 --> 00:19:03,170
historically grown, though with painful drops along

606
00:19:03,170 --> 00:19:03,680
the way.

607
00:19:04,031 --> 00:19:06,875
That said, some things people call investing

608
00:19:06,875 --> 00:19:08,814
really are closer to gambling.

609
00:19:08,994 --> 00:19:11,516
Betting your savings on one hot stock,

610
00:19:11,516 --> 00:19:13,972
trading options you don't understand,

611
00:19:13,972 --> 00:19:16,494
or chasing whatever went up last week.

612
00:19:16,674 --> 00:19:18,259
The difference is time,

613
00:19:18,259 --> 00:19:19,983
diversification and cost.

614
00:19:20,163 --> 00:19:22,145
Try this: if you invest,

615
00:19:22,145 --> 00:19:23,797
ask three questions.

616
00:19:23,977 --> 00:19:25,815
How many companies do I own through

617
00:19:25,815 --> 00:19:26,078
this?

618
00:19:26,258 --> 00:19:27,709
What does it cost per year?

619
00:19:27,889 --> 00:19:29,170
And can I hold it for ten

620
00:19:29,170 --> 00:19:30,502
years without touching it?

621
00:19:30,852 --> 00:19:33,290
The myth is strengthened every time a

622
00:19:33,290 --> 00:19:35,925
market crashes and the headlines scream.

623
00:19:36,105 --> 00:19:38,478
Drops of twenty or thirty percent are

624
00:19:38,478 --> 00:19:40,145
scary, and they do happen.

625
00:19:40,325 --> 00:19:42,667
But the difference between a gambler and

626
00:19:42,667 --> 00:19:44,424
an owner is what they do next.

627
00:19:44,604 --> 00:19:47,019
A gambler wants the money back fast.

628
00:19:47,199 --> 00:19:48,792
An owner keeps owning,

629
00:19:48,792 --> 00:19:50,531
keeps adding, and waits.

630
00:19:50,711 --> 00:19:51,916
Over long periods,

631
00:19:51,916 --> 00:19:54,595
patience has historically been rewarded,

632
00:19:54,595 --> 00:19:56,202
though never guaranteed.

633
00:19:56,553 --> 00:19:57,528
Quick pause.

634
00:19:57,708 --> 00:19:59,374
If one of these myths hit a

635
00:19:59,374 --> 00:20:00,670
little close to home,

636
00:20:00,670 --> 00:20:02,213
subscribe to the channel.

637
00:20:02,393 --> 00:20:04,326
It's free, and it helps me keep

638
00:20:04,326 --> 00:20:05,574
making these videos.

639
00:20:05,754 --> 00:20:06,931
Now the last angle,

640
00:20:06,931 --> 00:20:08,915
the one that changes what you do

641
00:20:08,915 --> 00:20:09,597
this month.

642
00:20:09,947 --> 00:20:10,806
Angle three.

643
00:20:10,986 --> 00:20:12,165
Myths about credit,

644
00:20:12,165 --> 00:20:14,646
debt and investing that change your next

645
00:20:14,646 --> 00:20:14,956
move.

646
00:20:15,307 --> 00:20:17,060
Myth number fifteen.

647
00:20:17,240 --> 00:20:19,124
You need to carry a credit card

648
00:20:19,124 --> 00:20:20,583
balance to build credit.

649
00:20:20,934 --> 00:20:23,065
This one costs Americans a lot of

650
00:20:23,065 --> 00:20:23,453
money.

651
00:20:23,633 --> 00:20:25,531
You don't need to carry a balance

652
00:20:25,531 --> 00:20:27,429
and pay interest to build credit.

653
00:20:27,609 --> 00:20:30,952
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau explains that

654
00:20:30,952 --> 00:20:33,428
paying on time and keeping your balances

655
00:20:33,428 --> 00:20:35,842
low compared with your limits are among

656
00:20:35,842 --> 00:20:37,513
the most important factors.

657
00:20:37,693 --> 00:20:40,300
Paying the full statement balance every month

658
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:41,922
shows you can handle credit,

659
00:20:41,922 --> 00:20:43,660
and costs nothing in interest.

660
00:20:44,011 --> 00:20:45,775
Here's the difference in dollars.

661
00:20:45,955 --> 00:20:48,589
Carrying a two thousand dollar balance at

662
00:20:48,589 --> 00:20:51,480
twenty two percent costs roughly four hundred

663
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:53,600
forty dollars a year in interest,

664
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:55,592
for zero benefit to your score.

665
00:20:55,772 --> 00:20:57,652
Paying in full costs nothing.

666
00:20:57,832 --> 00:21:00,217
Try this: set your card to automatically

667
00:21:00,217 --> 00:21:02,720
pay the full statement balance each month.

668
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:03,909
If you can't yet,

669
00:21:03,909 --> 00:21:05,572
set it to pay well above the

670
00:21:05,572 --> 00:21:06,047
minimum.

671
00:21:06,397 --> 00:21:07,918
Where did this myth come from?

672
00:21:08,098 --> 00:21:09,026
It's a mix up.

673
00:21:09,206 --> 00:21:11,580
Using a credit card regularly and paying

674
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:13,538
it on time does help build credit

675
00:21:13,538 --> 00:21:14,013
history.

676
00:21:14,193 --> 00:21:15,717
Somewhere along the way,

677
00:21:15,717 --> 00:21:17,940
using a card turned into carrying a

678
00:21:17,940 --> 00:21:19,591
balance in people's minds.

679
00:21:19,771 --> 00:21:20,665
They're not the same.

680
00:21:20,845 --> 00:21:22,754
You can use the card every month,

681
00:21:22,754 --> 00:21:24,027
pay it off completely,

682
00:21:24,027 --> 00:21:26,283
and get all the benefits without paying

683
00:21:26,283 --> 00:21:27,498
a dollar of interest.

684
00:21:27,848 --> 00:21:29,578
Myth number sixteen.

685
00:21:29,758 --> 00:21:31,975
Checking your own credit score hurts it.

686
00:21:32,325 --> 00:21:34,077
When you check your own credit,

687
00:21:34,077 --> 00:21:35,603
it's called a soft inquiry,

688
00:21:35,603 --> 00:21:37,468
and it doesn't affect your score.

689
00:21:37,648 --> 00:21:40,104
Hard inquiries happen when a lender checks

690
00:21:40,104 --> 00:21:42,385
your credit because you applied for new

691
00:21:42,385 --> 00:21:44,432
credit, and those can have a small,

692
00:21:44,432 --> 00:21:45,427
temporary effect.

693
00:21:45,607 --> 00:21:47,740
Looking at your own numbers is free

694
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:48,532
and harmless.

695
00:21:48,883 --> 00:21:51,414
People who avoid checking often miss errors.

696
00:21:51,594 --> 00:21:53,148
A wrong late payment,

697
00:21:53,148 --> 00:21:55,295
an account that isn't theirs,

698
00:21:55,295 --> 00:21:57,294
an old debt reported twice.

699
00:21:57,474 --> 00:21:59,863
Mistakes on credit reports aren't rare,

700
00:21:59,863 --> 00:22:02,130
and fixing them can raise your score.

701
00:22:02,310 --> 00:22:04,883
Try this: get your free reports from

702
00:22:04,883 --> 00:22:06,956
annual credit report dot com,

703
00:22:06,956 --> 00:22:08,242
the official site,

704
00:22:08,242 --> 00:22:10,530
and read every line once a year.

705
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:12,819
This myth comes from a half truth.

706
00:22:12,999 --> 00:22:15,086
Applying for lots of new credit in

707
00:22:15,086 --> 00:22:17,112
a short time can lower your score

708
00:22:17,112 --> 00:22:19,138
a bit, because lenders may see it

709
00:22:19,138 --> 00:22:20,243
as a sign of risk.

710
00:22:20,423 --> 00:22:22,700
People heard credit checks hurt your score,

711
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:24,394
and lumped every check together.

712
00:22:24,574 --> 00:22:26,720
But checking your own report is not

713
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:28,068
applying for anything.

714
00:22:28,248 --> 00:22:30,309
Many banks and card apps now show

715
00:22:30,309 --> 00:22:31,557
your score for free,

716
00:22:31,557 --> 00:22:33,868
and looking at it is completely safe.

717
00:22:34,218 --> 00:22:35,831
Myth number seventeen.

718
00:22:36,011 --> 00:22:39,251
Closing old credit cards improves your score.

719
00:22:39,601 --> 00:22:41,966
It feels responsible to close cards you

720
00:22:41,966 --> 00:22:42,573
don't use.

721
00:22:42,753 --> 00:22:43,925
But it can backfire.

722
00:22:44,105 --> 00:22:45,605
Your score looks at how much of

723
00:22:45,605 --> 00:22:47,298
your available credit you're using.

724
00:22:47,478 --> 00:22:48,344
Close a card,

725
00:22:48,344 --> 00:22:50,475
and your available credit drops,

726
00:22:50,475 --> 00:22:53,272
so the same balance suddenly looks bigger.

727
00:22:53,452 --> 00:22:55,774
Older accounts can also help the average

728
00:22:55,774 --> 00:22:57,341
age of your credit history.

729
00:22:57,691 --> 00:22:59,552
If an old card has no annual

730
00:22:59,552 --> 00:23:01,878
fee and doesn't tempt you to spend,

731
00:23:01,878 --> 00:23:03,938
it's often fine to keep it open

732
00:23:03,938 --> 00:23:05,999
and use it for a small purchase

733
00:23:05,999 --> 00:23:06,863
now and then.

734
00:23:07,043 --> 00:23:08,649
If it has a high annual fee

735
00:23:08,649 --> 00:23:10,076
or it really tempts you,

736
00:23:10,076 --> 00:23:12,039
closing it may still be the right

737
00:23:12,039 --> 00:23:12,337
call.

738
00:23:12,517 --> 00:23:14,972
Try this: before closing any card,

739
00:23:14,972 --> 00:23:17,500
check its fee and your total credit

740
00:23:17,500 --> 00:23:19,378
use, and decide with both.

741
00:23:19,728 --> 00:23:22,084
This myth comes from good instincts.

742
00:23:22,264 --> 00:23:24,853
Fewer cards can mean less temptation and

743
00:23:24,853 --> 00:23:25,759
less to track.

744
00:23:25,939 --> 00:23:27,356
For some people that's worth it.

745
00:23:27,536 --> 00:23:29,103
The key is knowing the trade off.

746
00:23:29,283 --> 00:23:30,654
If you close a card,

747
00:23:30,654 --> 00:23:33,054
try to keep your other balances low

748
00:23:33,054 --> 00:23:35,866
so your overall credit use stays healthy.

749
00:23:36,046 --> 00:23:38,494
And never close your oldest card right

750
00:23:38,494 --> 00:23:40,878
before applying for a mortgage or car

751
00:23:40,878 --> 00:23:41,201
loan.

752
00:23:41,551 --> 00:23:43,118
Myth number eighteen.

753
00:23:43,298 --> 00:23:44,656
All debt is bad.

754
00:23:45,007 --> 00:23:46,609
Debt is a tool.

755
00:23:46,789 --> 00:23:48,712
A low rate mortgage on a home

756
00:23:48,712 --> 00:23:49,707
you can afford,

757
00:23:49,707 --> 00:23:52,228
a reasonable student loan for a degree

758
00:23:52,228 --> 00:23:53,820
that raises your income,

759
00:23:53,820 --> 00:23:55,876
or a business loan with a clear

760
00:23:55,876 --> 00:23:57,203
plan can make sense.

761
00:23:57,383 --> 00:23:59,666
Credit card debt at twenty percent for

762
00:23:59,666 --> 00:24:01,588
things that lose value is a very

763
00:24:01,588 --> 00:24:02,549
different thing.

764
00:24:02,729 --> 00:24:03,967
The interest rate,

765
00:24:03,967 --> 00:24:05,617
and what the money buys,

766
00:24:05,617 --> 00:24:08,024
decide whether debt helps or hurts.

767
00:24:08,374 --> 00:24:09,569
A simple test.

768
00:24:09,749 --> 00:24:11,837
Does this debt help me earn more,

769
00:24:11,837 --> 00:24:13,989
or own something that holds value,

770
00:24:13,989 --> 00:24:16,393
at a reasonable rate I can comfortably

771
00:24:16,393 --> 00:24:16,646
pay?

772
00:24:16,826 --> 00:24:18,625
If yes, it might be useful.

773
00:24:18,805 --> 00:24:20,942
If it's for something that's gone in

774
00:24:20,942 --> 00:24:22,367
a month, at a high rate,

775
00:24:22,367 --> 00:24:23,554
it's usually a trap.

776
00:24:23,734 --> 00:24:26,228
Try this: label every debt you have

777
00:24:26,228 --> 00:24:27,368
as tool or trap.

778
00:24:27,548 --> 00:24:28,999
Pay off the traps first.

779
00:24:29,349 --> 00:24:31,644
This myth often comes from people who

780
00:24:31,644 --> 00:24:33,257
were badly burned by debt,

781
00:24:33,257 --> 00:24:35,119
and their caution makes sense.

782
00:24:35,299 --> 00:24:37,326
But fear of all debt can lead

783
00:24:37,326 --> 00:24:38,724
to its own mistakes,

784
00:24:38,724 --> 00:24:41,520
like draining every dollar of savings to

785
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:43,058
avoid a low rate loan,

786
00:24:43,058 --> 00:24:45,504
leaving no cushion for emergencies.

787
00:24:45,684 --> 00:24:47,751
Being debt free is a great goal.

788
00:24:47,931 --> 00:24:50,315
Being debt free with zero savings is

789
00:24:50,315 --> 00:24:50,845
fragile.

790
00:24:51,025 --> 00:24:52,174
Balance matters.

791
00:24:52,524 --> 00:24:54,079
Myth number nineteen.

792
00:24:54,259 --> 00:24:55,618
You need a lot of money to

793
00:24:55,618 --> 00:24:56,454
start investing.

794
00:24:56,804 --> 00:25:00,743
Decades ago, investing often required large minimums

795
00:25:00,743 --> 00:25:02,713
and expensive commissions.

796
00:25:02,893 --> 00:25:05,690
Today, many brokerages have no minimum to

797
00:25:05,690 --> 00:25:08,692
open an account and offer fractional shares,

798
00:25:08,692 --> 00:25:10,397
so you can buy a slice of

799
00:25:10,397 --> 00:25:12,239
a fund with a small amount.

800
00:25:12,419 --> 00:25:15,671
Many workplace retirement plans let you contribute

801
00:25:15,671 --> 00:25:17,818
a small percent of each paycheck.

802
00:25:18,168 --> 00:25:20,420
Starting small isn't just possible.

803
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:21,239
It's smart.

804
00:25:21,419 --> 00:25:23,340
It builds the habit and teaches you

805
00:25:23,340 --> 00:25:25,097
how markets feel when they move,

806
00:25:25,097 --> 00:25:26,469
while the stakes are low.

807
00:25:26,649 --> 00:25:28,815
Try this: if your employer offers a

808
00:25:28,815 --> 00:25:30,610
retirement plan with a match,

809
00:25:30,610 --> 00:25:32,899
contribute at least enough to get the

810
00:25:32,899 --> 00:25:33,580
full match.

811
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:35,397
That match is part of your pay.

812
00:25:35,747 --> 00:25:38,045
This myth hurts younger people the most.

813
00:25:38,225 --> 00:25:39,971
They wait to have a big amount

814
00:25:39,971 --> 00:25:42,298
before starting, and lose the years when

815
00:25:42,298 --> 00:25:43,578
time is most powerful.

816
00:25:43,758 --> 00:25:45,938
The first investment isn't really about the

817
00:25:45,938 --> 00:25:46,242
money.

818
00:25:46,422 --> 00:25:47,363
It's about learning.

819
00:25:47,543 --> 00:25:49,114
How to open an account,

820
00:25:49,114 --> 00:25:51,027
how to pick a low cost fund,

821
00:25:51,027 --> 00:25:53,487
how it feels when the balance drops.

822
00:25:53,667 --> 00:25:55,541
Better to learn those lessons with one

823
00:25:55,541 --> 00:25:57,858
hundred dollars than with one hundred thousand.

824
00:25:58,208 --> 00:25:59,508
Myth number twenty.

825
00:25:59,688 --> 00:26:01,977
You need a financial expert before you

826
00:26:01,977 --> 00:26:02,579
can start.

827
00:26:02,929 --> 00:26:04,927
Good advice can be valuable,

828
00:26:04,927 --> 00:26:08,495
especially for complex situations like a business,

829
00:26:08,495 --> 00:26:10,208
an inheritance or taxes.

830
00:26:10,388 --> 00:26:12,757
But the basics don't require an expert.

831
00:26:12,937 --> 00:26:14,197
An emergency fund,

832
00:26:14,197 --> 00:26:15,737
no high interest debt,

833
00:26:15,737 --> 00:26:19,097
and steady contributions to low cost diversified

834
00:26:19,097 --> 00:26:21,267
funds is a plan many people can

835
00:26:21,267 --> 00:26:22,457
run on their own.

836
00:26:22,807 --> 00:26:24,084
If you do hire help,

837
00:26:24,084 --> 00:26:25,489
know how they're paid.

838
00:26:25,669 --> 00:26:27,583
Ask whether they're a fiduciary,

839
00:26:27,583 --> 00:26:29,916
meaning legally required to act in your

840
00:26:29,916 --> 00:26:30,754
best interest.

841
00:26:30,934 --> 00:26:32,424
Ask about every fee,

842
00:26:32,424 --> 00:26:33,244
in dollars.

843
00:26:33,424 --> 00:26:35,755
Try this: write a one page plan

844
00:26:35,755 --> 00:26:36,733
for yourself.

845
00:26:36,913 --> 00:26:38,401
What you're saving for,

846
00:26:38,401 --> 00:26:39,696
how much each month,

847
00:26:39,696 --> 00:26:40,860
and where it goes.

848
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:42,489
If you can't fill it in,

849
00:26:42,489 --> 00:26:44,300
that's when an hour with a fee

850
00:26:44,300 --> 00:26:46,172
only advisor might be worth it.

851
00:26:46,522 --> 00:26:48,461
Part of this myth is marketing.

852
00:26:48,641 --> 00:26:50,069
Complexity sells.

853
00:26:50,249 --> 00:26:51,959
If investing looked simple,

854
00:26:51,959 --> 00:26:53,987
fewer people would pay for help.

855
00:26:54,167 --> 00:26:56,538
Some of the most respected investors,

856
00:26:56,538 --> 00:26:58,139
including Warren Buffett,

857
00:26:58,139 --> 00:27:00,125
have said that for most people,

858
00:27:00,125 --> 00:27:02,368
low cost index funds are a sensible

859
00:27:02,368 --> 00:27:02,816
choice.

860
00:27:02,996 --> 00:27:05,214
That doesn't mean advice is useless.

861
00:27:05,394 --> 00:27:07,331
It means simple is not the same

862
00:27:07,331 --> 00:27:08,018
as amateur.

863
00:27:08,368 --> 00:27:09,958
Breaking a myth isn't enough.

864
00:27:10,138 --> 00:27:11,845
You need something to replace it.

865
00:27:12,025 --> 00:27:14,437
So here are ten simple rules to

866
00:27:14,437 --> 00:27:15,682
live by instead.

867
00:27:16,032 --> 00:27:16,728
Rule one.

868
00:27:16,908 --> 00:27:19,449
Small amounts, repeated and invested,

869
00:27:19,449 --> 00:27:21,715
are how most people build wealth.

870
00:27:21,895 --> 00:27:22,754
Rule two.

871
00:27:22,934 --> 00:27:24,249
Cash for emergencies,

872
00:27:24,249 --> 00:27:26,127
investments for the long term.

873
00:27:26,307 --> 00:27:27,317
Rule three.

874
00:27:27,497 --> 00:27:29,533
Your home is a place to live

875
00:27:29,533 --> 00:27:29,970
first.

876
00:27:30,150 --> 00:27:30,893
Rule four.

877
00:27:31,073 --> 00:27:33,415
Compare the full costs of renting and

878
00:27:33,415 --> 00:27:34,997
owning before you decide.

879
00:27:35,347 --> 00:27:36,217
Rule five.

880
00:27:36,397 --> 00:27:38,740
Plan for Social Security to be part

881
00:27:38,740 --> 00:27:40,012
of your retirement,

882
00:27:40,012 --> 00:27:40,949
not all of it.

883
00:27:41,129 --> 00:27:42,034
Rule six.

884
00:27:42,214 --> 00:27:44,341
A big tax refund is a loan

885
00:27:44,341 --> 00:27:45,813
you gave for free.

886
00:27:45,993 --> 00:27:46,806
Rule seven.

887
00:27:46,986 --> 00:27:48,539
Invest on a schedule,

888
00:27:48,539 --> 00:27:49,796
not on a feeling.

889
00:27:50,146 --> 00:27:50,877
Rule eight.

890
00:27:51,057 --> 00:27:53,054
Build the habit before the income.

891
00:27:53,234 --> 00:27:54,023
Rule nine.

892
00:27:54,203 --> 00:27:55,919
Pay your cards in full and check

893
00:27:55,919 --> 00:27:57,152
your credit every year.

894
00:27:57,332 --> 00:27:58,308
And rule ten.

895
00:27:58,488 --> 00:27:59,535
Keep it simple,

896
00:27:59,535 --> 00:28:00,513
keep it cheap,

897
00:28:00,513 --> 00:28:02,608
and keep going for a very long

898
00:28:02,608 --> 00:28:02,958
time.

899
00:28:03,308 --> 00:28:04,724
Let's put a price on a few

900
00:28:04,724 --> 00:28:06,686
of these myths with simple examples.

901
00:28:06,866 --> 00:28:07,621
Take Kevin.

902
00:28:07,801 --> 00:28:10,269
He believed small amounts don't matter,

903
00:28:10,269 --> 00:28:12,358
so for ten years he never started

904
00:28:12,358 --> 00:28:12,990
investing.

905
00:28:13,170 --> 00:28:15,521
When he finally began at thirty five,

906
00:28:15,521 --> 00:28:17,363
he had to save much more each

907
00:28:17,363 --> 00:28:19,333
month to reach the same goal as

908
00:28:19,333 --> 00:28:21,556
a friend who started at twenty five

909
00:28:21,556 --> 00:28:22,191
with less.

910
00:28:22,371 --> 00:28:24,299
The myth didn't feel expensive.

911
00:28:24,479 --> 00:28:26,940
It cost him a decade of compounding.

912
00:28:27,290 --> 00:28:28,265
Then there's Priya.

913
00:28:28,445 --> 00:28:31,156
She thought carrying a small balance helped

914
00:28:31,156 --> 00:28:33,173
her credit, so she always left a

915
00:28:33,173 --> 00:28:35,190
few hundred dollars on her card.

916
00:28:35,370 --> 00:28:36,419
Over five years,

917
00:28:36,419 --> 00:28:39,042
she paid hundreds of dollars in interest

918
00:28:39,042 --> 00:28:39,829
for nothing.

919
00:28:40,009 --> 00:28:42,121
When she switched to paying in full,

920
00:28:42,121 --> 00:28:43,587
her score kept improving,

921
00:28:43,587 --> 00:28:45,523
and her interest dropped to zero.

922
00:28:45,874 --> 00:28:48,245
And Tom and Rachel believed renting was

923
00:28:48,245 --> 00:28:49,461
throwing money away.

924
00:28:49,641 --> 00:28:51,160
They stretched to buy a house at

925
00:28:51,160 --> 00:28:52,300
the top of their budget.

926
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:55,355
Between taxes, insurance and a roof repair

927
00:28:55,355 --> 00:28:56,176
in year two,

928
00:28:56,176 --> 00:28:57,887
they had no cushion left,

929
00:28:57,887 --> 00:29:00,351
and put the repairs on credit cards.

930
00:29:00,531 --> 00:29:01,936
Owning wasn't the mistake.

931
00:29:02,116 --> 00:29:03,858
Buying with no margin was.

932
00:29:04,038 --> 00:29:05,219
A few years later,

933
00:29:05,219 --> 00:29:07,254
after building savings back up,

934
00:29:07,254 --> 00:29:08,960
they were glad they owned.

935
00:29:09,140 --> 00:29:09,953
The order mattered.

936
00:29:10,303 --> 00:29:11,348
Notice the pattern.

937
00:29:11,528 --> 00:29:13,315
None of these people were careless.

938
00:29:13,495 --> 00:29:15,574
They were following advice that sounded right.

939
00:29:15,754 --> 00:29:17,797
That's what makes myths so expensive.

940
00:29:17,977 --> 00:29:19,184
The fix is simple:

941
00:29:19,184 --> 00:29:21,263
whenever you hear a money rule,

942
00:29:21,263 --> 00:29:23,074
ask for the math behind it.

943
00:29:23,424 --> 00:29:25,628
A few questions people ask me about

944
00:29:25,628 --> 00:29:26,384
these myths.

945
00:29:26,734 --> 00:29:27,593
Question one.

946
00:29:27,773 --> 00:29:29,410
Where do money myths come from?

947
00:29:29,590 --> 00:29:31,436
Mostly from good intentions.

948
00:29:31,616 --> 00:29:33,528
Parents repeat what worked for them,

949
00:29:33,528 --> 00:29:34,750
in a different economy.

950
00:29:34,930 --> 00:29:37,461
Salespeople repeat what helps them sell.

951
00:29:37,641 --> 00:29:39,726
And social media rewards bold,

952
00:29:39,726 --> 00:29:41,949
simple claims over careful ones.

953
00:29:42,129 --> 00:29:44,600
The best defense is checking a claim

954
00:29:44,600 --> 00:29:46,315
against a neutral source,

955
00:29:46,315 --> 00:29:49,198
like a government agency or an independent

956
00:29:49,198 --> 00:29:49,884
nonprofit.

957
00:29:50,234 --> 00:29:51,116
Question two.

958
00:29:51,296 --> 00:29:52,932
Is it ever smart to keep a

959
00:29:52,932 --> 00:29:53,688
lot of cash?

960
00:29:53,868 --> 00:29:54,251
Yes.

961
00:29:54,431 --> 00:29:56,672
Your emergency fund should be in cash.

962
00:29:56,852 --> 00:29:58,835
And money you'll need in the next

963
00:29:58,835 --> 00:30:00,699
few years, like a down payment,

964
00:30:00,699 --> 00:30:02,924
usually belongs somewhere stable too.

965
00:30:03,104 --> 00:30:05,228
The myth isn't that cash is bad.

966
00:30:05,408 --> 00:30:07,266
It's that cash is the best place

967
00:30:07,266 --> 00:30:08,427
for long term money.

968
00:30:08,777 --> 00:30:09,914
Question three.

969
00:30:10,094 --> 00:30:11,623
If renting can be fine,

970
00:30:11,623 --> 00:30:13,485
when does buying make sense?

971
00:30:13,665 --> 00:30:15,730
Generally when you plan to stay for

972
00:30:15,730 --> 00:30:17,973
many years, you have a solid emergency

973
00:30:17,973 --> 00:30:19,625
fund after the down payment,

974
00:30:19,625 --> 00:30:21,691
and the full monthly cost of owning

975
00:30:21,691 --> 00:30:23,579
fits comfortably in your budget.

976
00:30:23,759 --> 00:30:26,140
Many people use online rent versus buy

977
00:30:26,140 --> 00:30:28,896
calculators to compare the numbers for their

978
00:30:28,896 --> 00:30:29,460
own city.

979
00:30:29,810 --> 00:30:30,820
Question four.

980
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:32,334
Is it bad to get a tax

981
00:30:32,334 --> 00:30:33,182
refund at all?

982
00:30:33,362 --> 00:30:33,571
No.

983
00:30:33,751 --> 00:30:35,601
A small refund is normal,

984
00:30:35,601 --> 00:30:38,117
because exact withholding is hard.

985
00:30:38,297 --> 00:30:40,232
The issue is a very large refund

986
00:30:40,232 --> 00:30:42,650
every year while you carry high interest

987
00:30:42,650 --> 00:30:42,952
debt.

988
00:30:43,132 --> 00:30:45,484
That's money working for the government instead

989
00:30:45,484 --> 00:30:46,035
of for you.

990
00:30:46,385 --> 00:30:47,662
And question five.

991
00:30:47,842 --> 00:30:50,405
What's the single most expensive myth on

992
00:30:50,405 --> 00:30:51,046
this list?

993
00:30:51,226 --> 00:30:52,374
For most people,

994
00:30:52,374 --> 00:30:53,595
it's number five.

995
00:30:53,775 --> 00:30:57,493
Assuming Social Security will cover retirement leads

996
00:30:57,493 --> 00:30:58,994
to saving too little,

997
00:30:58,994 --> 00:30:59,638
too late.

998
00:30:59,818 --> 00:31:02,569
The second most expensive is probably number

999
00:31:02,569 --> 00:31:05,195
one, believing small amounts don't matter,

1000
00:31:05,195 --> 00:31:06,946
because it delays the start.

1001
00:31:07,126 --> 00:31:08,763
And in personal finance,

1002
00:31:08,763 --> 00:31:10,400
the start is everything.

1003
00:31:10,750 --> 00:31:12,503
One last thought on myths.

1004
00:31:12,683 --> 00:31:14,677
Not every piece of old advice is

1005
00:31:14,677 --> 00:31:15,051
wrong.

1006
00:31:15,231 --> 00:31:16,601
Spend less than you earn.

1007
00:31:16,781 --> 00:31:18,360
Avoid debt you can't afford.

1008
00:31:18,540 --> 00:31:20,038
Save for a rainy day.

1009
00:31:20,218 --> 00:31:21,141
Those are old,

1010
00:31:21,141 --> 00:31:22,261
and they're true.

1011
00:31:22,441 --> 00:31:24,625
The skill isn't rejecting everything you were

1012
00:31:24,625 --> 00:31:24,868
told.

1013
00:31:25,048 --> 00:31:27,517
It's keeping the advice that survives the

1014
00:31:27,517 --> 00:31:29,204
math, and dropping the rest.

1015
00:31:29,554 --> 00:31:30,297
Quick recap.

1016
00:31:30,477 --> 00:31:31,509
The math myths:

1017
00:31:31,509 --> 00:31:33,368
small amounts don't matter,

1018
00:31:33,368 --> 00:31:34,400
cash is safest,

1019
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:36,809
your house is your best investment,

1020
00:31:36,809 --> 00:31:38,943
renting is throwing money away,

1021
00:31:38,943 --> 00:31:41,077
Social Security will cover you,

1022
00:31:41,077 --> 00:31:43,073
the tax refund is free money,

1023
00:31:43,073 --> 00:31:45,001
and you can time the market.

1024
00:31:45,351 --> 00:31:46,656
The mindset myths:

1025
00:31:46,656 --> 00:31:48,396
more income will fix it,

1026
00:31:48,396 --> 00:31:50,354
rich people just got lucky,

1027
00:31:50,354 --> 00:31:52,457
budgets are for broke people,

1028
00:31:52,457 --> 00:31:54,488
talking about money is rude,

1029
00:31:54,488 --> 00:31:56,519
you're too young or too old,

1030
00:31:56,519 --> 00:31:57,896
frugal means cheap,

1031
00:31:57,896 --> 00:31:59,782
and investing is gambling.

1032
00:32:00,132 --> 00:32:01,745
And the playbook myths:

1033
00:32:01,745 --> 00:32:03,990
carry a balance to build credit,

1034
00:32:03,990 --> 00:32:06,024
checking your score hurts it,

1035
00:32:06,024 --> 00:32:07,147
close old cards,

1036
00:32:07,147 --> 00:32:08,269
all debt is bad,

1037
00:32:08,269 --> 00:32:10,654
you need a lot to start investing,

1038
00:32:10,654 --> 00:32:12,969
and you need an expert before you

1039
00:32:12,969 --> 00:32:13,390
begin.

1040
00:32:13,741 --> 00:32:15,490
If you want the full plan without

1041
00:32:15,490 --> 00:32:17,346
the myths, from broke to your first

1042
00:32:17,346 --> 00:32:18,884
million, it's in my playbook.

1043
00:32:19,064 --> 00:32:20,689
The link is in the description.

1044
00:32:20,869 --> 00:32:22,746
And if you haven't seen it yet,

1045
00:32:22,746 --> 00:32:24,743
watch my video on why most people

1046
00:32:24,743 --> 00:32:25,409
stay broke.

1047
00:32:25,589 --> 00:32:27,620
It pairs perfectly with this one.

1048
00:32:27,800 --> 00:32:29,124
I'm Ryan Rover.

1049
00:32:29,304 --> 00:32:30,488
See you in the next one.
